The representative seed for all learned qualitative curves was fixed at 42 before final aggregation. It was selected as a central seed in the earlier six-route screening rather than as the best final case. Quantitative conclusions use all ten seeds and all six formal routes; qualitative figures cannot replace those summaries.

\subsection{Metrics and Statistical Analysis}

Offline primary metrics were grade mean absolute error (MAE) and either the registered $|\theta|\leq10^{\circ}$ P95 absolute error or the edge-region P95 absolute error, depending on the frozen contrast. Closed-loop primary raw metrics were lateral-error RMS, heading-error RMS, position-error RMS, the composite input-increment index $J_{\Delta u}$, and yaw-rate-command RMS. Lower values favor the target method. The composite input-increment index is a control-regularity summary, not a physical energy measure.

Paired effects are reported as comparator minus target so that positive values favor the target. Offline intervals resampled paired model seeds. Learned closed-loop intervals resampled model seeds first and then retained all six matched routes within each resampled seed; deterministic comparisons resampled routes. All reported bootstrap intervals used 10000 iterations and seed 20260715. Table~\ref{tab:evaluation_protocol} records the unit and interpretation of each evidence family.

\begin{table*}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Evaluation metrics, statistical units, and interpretation rules.}
\label{tab:evaluation_protocol}
\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.0pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.19\textwidth}p{0.25\textwidth}p{0.16\textwidth}p{0.16\textwidth}p{0.16\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Evidence family & Reported quantity & Unit & Statistical unit & Decision rule \\
\midrule
Offline grade estimation & MAE; registered P95 absolute error & deg & Ten paired model seeds & Positive comparator-minus-target effect favors target \\
Deterministic closed loop & $e_y$ RMS; $e_\psi$ RMS; $J_{\Delta u}$ & m; rad; -- & Six paired routes & Route bootstrap; CI relative to zero effect \\
Learned closed loop & Same raw metrics plus scheduled-grade MAE & m; rad; --; deg & Ten seeds, six routes/seed & Hierarchical seed-first bootstrap \\
Fusion development & Grade-error and control-index ratios & ratio & 90 paired cases across nine routes and ten seeds & Aggregate CI plus pre-registered consistency checks \\
Failure audit & Gate pass, fail, or not adjudicable & status & Registered contrast/case & Missing inputs or zero denominators are never coerced to pass \\
Runtime & p50, p95, p99, maximum, $>10$-ms rate & ms & 10000 timed calls after 500 warm-ups & Compare methods only within the same backend and scope \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\subsection{Reproducibility, Protocol Lock, and Reporting Boundaries}

The data file, contract, model checkpoints, plant revision, six route files, LPV database, controller cache, metrics, thresholds, and seed set were identified by frozen paths and hashes. Completeness audits found 40/40 offline estimator rows, 240/240 estimator closed-loop rows, 18/18 scheduling-necessity cases, and 138/138 five-source controller cases. A source cross-check covered 38 confidence intervals with no discrepancy.

The A5 statistical audit nevertheless remained \texttt{PARTIAL}. Seven registered contrasts contained safety-gate fields that were missing or had zero-denominator comparators, so those gates were marked not adjudicable. This status is reported separately from the estimable performance effects. No physical-vehicle or hardware-in-the-loop test, template-disjoint dataset split, formal stability proof, or new robustness experiment is implied by the protocol.

\section{Results and Discussion}
\label{sec:results_discussion}

\subsection{RQ1: Does Accurate Grade Scheduling Affect the Frozen LPV-MPC?}

The truth-driven conditioned reference reduced all three primary route-level outcomes relative to zero-grade scheduling. Across six routes, the mean reductions were 1.4680~m for lateral-error RMS, 0.33065~rad for heading-error RMS, and 695.59 for the composite input-increment index; all corresponding 95\% intervals were above zero (Table~\ref{tab:slope_necessity_effects}). The simple causal-IMU baseline reduced lateral-error RMS by 0.06906~m, but its heading and increment-index intervals crossed zero. Thus, the controller can benefit strongly from accurate grade scheduling, while merely supplying a causal inertial proxy does not reproduce the truth-driven reference.

