\subsection{RQ3: Does Offline Improvement Transfer Selectively to Closed-Loop Control?}

The ModernTCN-delta advantage over ModernTCN-22D transferred selectively. Its mean lateral- and heading-error effects were favorable, but both confidence intervals included zero, whereas the reduction in $J_{\Delta u}$ was 15.912 with a 95\% interval of [1.638, 55.899] (Table~\ref{tab:estimator_and_delta_results}). Relative to GRU-22D and TCN-22D, the registered estimator closed-loop effects were supported on the three primary metrics, but full pre-registered qualification was not established because the associated safety-gate audit did not pass.

The 24 of 60 ModernTCN-delta closed-loop rows marked as gate failures were repetitions of four seed-level offline failures across six paths, not 24 independent unstable simulations. This distinction prevents a model-qualification gate from being misreported as a closed-loop instability count. More generally, the result shows why offline slope error cannot be used as a surrogate for every controller outcome: the scheduler, constrained optimizer, route geometry, and command regularization transform perception errors nonlinearly.

\subsection{RQ4: Is the Causal Inertial Observer a Qualified Complementary Source?}

The qualified causal inertial observer was evaluated over 90 cases. It obtained a mean MAE of 0.19985$^{\circ}$, mean P95 absolute error of 0.49109$^{\circ}$, mean bias of $-0.01079^{\circ}$, mean invalid fraction of 1.75\%, and mean safe-beneficial fraction of 44.20\%. All frozen observer qualification checks passed. This observer is distinct from the simple causal-IMU baseline used in RQ1: the latter is a deliberately simple scheduling source, whereas the former is the qualified branch supplied to the adaptive fusion interface.

Figure~\ref{fig:imu_observer_trace} fixes the P3 long-up/down, seed-42 case and the full 4401-sample record. The final trace must show invalid intervals and transient disagreement rather than only a favorable segment.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\fbox{\parbox[c][1.75in][c]{0.93\columnwidth}{\centering\footnotesize
\texttt{TODO-FIG-FINAL} -- R3 / single-column / aligned trace panels\\[4pt]
Scientific question: Where does the qualified causal inertial observer complement or disagree with the learned estimate?\\
Data identifiers: Node40 IMU\_ONLY; P3 long up/down; seed 42; complete 4401 samples.}}
\caption{Planned qualified causal inertial-observer trace for P3 at seed 42. The final figure will retain the full case, validity state, reference grade, observer output, and absolute error.}
\label{fig:imu_observer_trace}
\end{figure}

The observer evidence supports availability of complementary causal information, not direct replacement of ModernTCN-delta. Similar separation of model-based and measurement-driven information has been used to manage grade-estimation uncertainty \citep{Gao2024RoadSlopeHDV}; here, the relevant conclusion is narrower and follows from the frozen observer qualification only.

\subsection{RQ4 Continued: Does Uncertainty-Adaptive Fusion Convert Complementarity into Benefit?}

The nine-pair pilot reduced the mean scheduled-grade error ratio to 0.83615, but its mean control ratio was 0.97205 with a 95\% upper bound of 1.04678; therefore, the pilot did not qualify the complete control claim. A fixed-correction ablation further showed a mean control ratio of 1.01737 and a worst P3 seed-42 ratio of 1.46648, demonstrating the route--seed sensitivity of a constant correction strength.

In the 90-pair Node42 development set, adaptive fusion produced a mean scheduled-grade error ratio of 0.83597 (upper bound 0.85603) and a mean control-index ratio of 0.95406 (upper bound 0.97864). Correction was active at 33.08\% of samples. Among active samples, 70.52\% improved absolute grade error by more than 0.1$^{\circ}$, 29.07\% remained within the $\pm0.1^{\circ}$ neutral band, and 0.41\% degraded by more than 0.1$^{\circ}$. Exact fallback was verified when the inertial input was invalid, and no recorded safety failure occurred. Table~\ref{tab:observer_fusion_evidence} retains both the average benefits and the failed strict case-consistency checks.

