The paired worst cases were metric dependent rather than concentrated in one route--seed pair. This pattern supports hierarchical reporting and cautions against selecting a single representative trajectory after viewing the final ranking. It also explains why a positive grand mean can coexist with failed strict all-case consistency criteria.

\subsection{Is Core Inference Fast, and What Does the Full MATLAB Cycle Cost?}

Under the common ONNX Runtime CPU backend, batch size one, one thread, 500 warm-ups, and 10000 timed calls, ModernTCN-delta had a p95 core inference time of 0.4038~ms with no call above 10~ms. GRU-22D was the fastest core predictor, and TCN-22D produced one call above 10~ms. These within-backend results support core inference feasibility, not a claim that ModernTCN-delta has minimum latency.

The MATLAB-native scopes lead to a different conclusion. ModernTCN-delta core inference had a p95 of 12.1382~ms, its end-to-end estimator update had a p95 of 13.3069~ms, the effective controller solve had a p95 of 23.1211~ms, and the measured slope-estimation-plus-MPC cycle had a p95 of 52.2069~ms. Table~\ref{tab:runtime_scope} therefore separates backends and interfaces. The fusion wrapper was not eligible at the A2 runtime freeze and is not retroactively assigned a timing value.

