Accordingly, only the same-backend neural inference core demonstrated margin relative to the 10-ms sampling period. The present MATLAB chain did not demonstrate complete 100-Hz execution; code generation, data-transfer optimization, memory reuse, solver optimization, and integrated real-time tests remain necessary.

\subsection{Integrated Interpretation, Failure Evidence, and Scope}

The evidence closes the five research questions with different strengths. Accurate truth-driven scheduling produced large route-level effects, and ModernTCN-delta improved the registered offline metrics. The additional representation benefit transferred clearly to the composite input-increment index but not conclusively to both tracking-error metrics. The qualified causal inertial observer supplied complementary information, and adaptive fusion improved aggregate grade and control-index ratios, yet its strict case-consistency criteria failed. In the formal five-source comparison, Fusion-LPV-MPC improved scheduled-grade MAE and heading-error RMS relative to MTCN-LPV-MPC, while lateral-error and increment-index intervals crossed zero.

The negative and conditional evidence is part of the result. A constant correction could substantially degrade one route--seed case; P3 and P6 contained adverse route-level outcomes; four seed-level offline gates generated 24 repeated failed rows; and seven registered safety contrasts were not adjudicable under the frozen A5 rules. Zero recorded solver failures and exact fusion fallback are useful observations, but they are not substitutes for those unavailable safety decisions.

The study is limited to same-domain simulation with a run-disjoint but non-template-disjoint dataset split. It includes neither physical-vehicle nor hardware-in-the-loop validation, and it does not establish formal closed-loop stability or safety guarantees. The timing evidence further confines the real-time claim to the tested inference core. Within those boundaries, the results support a perception-to-scheduling interface in which learned temporal estimation remains primary, inertial information is applied selectively, and the constrained LPV-MPC continues to generate the commands.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This study developed and evaluated a slope-aware perception-to-control chain for a diagonal dual-drive-and-steer AGV. The chain combines a 22D causal proprioceptive input, a multi-scale delta bank, ModernTCN-delta grade estimation, a qualified causal inertial observer, bounded uncertainty-adaptive correction, grade conditioning, and the frozen LPV-MPC. The learning modules provide an interpretable scheduling variable rather than replacing the vehicle model, optimizer, or control law.

The strongest evidence concerns the value of accurate grade scheduling and offline estimation. Relative to zero-grade LPV-MPC, the truth-driven conditioned reference reduced lateral-error RMS, heading-error RMS, and the composite input-increment index across the six-route contrast. ModernTCN-delta obtained the lowest ten-seed mean MAE and registered P95 error among the four tested estimators, and its paired offline advantages over ModernTCN-22D, GRU-22D, and TCN-22D were supported. The delta-bank advantage over ModernTCN-22D transferred clearly to the composite input-increment index, whereas its lateral- and heading-error intervals crossed zero.

The fusion evidence is conditional. The qualified causal inertial observer passed its frozen development criteria, and the 90-pair adaptive development set showed favorable aggregate grade-error and control-index ratios with exact invalid-input fallback. However, strict case-consistency criteria were not met. In the formal 60-pair Fusion-LPV-MPC comparison, scheduled-grade MAE and heading-error RMS improved relative to MTCN-LPV-MPC, while lateral-error RMS and the composite input-increment index remained inconclusive. These results support selective average benefit rather than uniform superiority across routes, seeds, activations, or control metrics.

The conclusions apply to the frozen same-domain simulation protocol. The split was run-disjoint but not template-disjoint, no physical-vehicle or hardware-in-the-loop experiment was performed, and no formal stability or safety guarantee was established. Same-backend ONNX core inference showed substantial margin relative to 10~ms, but the measured MATLAB slope-estimation-plus-MPC cycle had a p95 of 52.2069~ms and did not demonstrate complete 100-Hz deployment. Future implementation work must therefore address the integrated runtime path and validate the method under independently collected physical data before broader deployment claims are made.

